GRC for cybersecurity can often seem complex, but it's far from it.

In this series of posts, we're going to understand everything this universe involves, from creating policies and recommended tools, to audit processes and everything you need to know to master GRC.

## But why does this matter?

GRC stands for Governance, Risk and Compliance, which in Portuguese we translate as Governança, Riscos e Conformidade.

(A good acronym works even in two languages.)

Let's understand each of these terms:

**Governance:** This is the foundation of the entire GRC process. Governance is what defines the processes and policies, as well as the use of resources, the organization and the structure of everything involved in management. It's the guiding star of GRC.

**Risk:** This word is already more common in our day to day. Risk is, well… risk. The risk of something happening, the treatment of that risk, what the areas are willing to do (or not do), what's expected and what we're going to face tomorrow. Since we're already more used to the concept, it's relatively easy to bring it into the cybersecurity context. But know that this part also involves several more specialized processes, which we'll explore throughout this series. Risk has several stages in its flow, involving identification, classification, treatment and response.

**Compliance:** Last but not least, compliance is a term auditors love. It's directly tied to the concept of being "in compliance". In other words: considering all the processes, risks, people, systems, software, hardware, policies and actions… are we doing everything the right way? What do we need to adjust to get squared away? Compliance is basically the control over the two previous pillars. It's through compliance that we know whether we're properly following our policies (governance) and whether we're adequately treating the dangers (risk). Compliance determines whether or not you're within what's expected, but it also depends on governance, on the risks identified, on the applicable standards and on current legislation.

Now that we understand what each of these points represents, let's understand why all of this is so important. A solid GRC process is fundamental these days to guarantee a good security posture. And the bigger the company, the more robust and mature its GRC needs to be.

Without it, your entire environment, finance, cybersecurity, IT, back office, and any other function you can imagine, ends up isolated, poorly connected and exposed to improvisation. And when we're talking about cybersecurity (our focus here), improvisation is synonymous with death for business continuity. (And yes, we're going to talk about that too.)

Let's think about the following scenario: You've just found out that one of the computers in the IT area has been breached. The reason? An admin user with weak credentials.

From there, the attacker can do whatever they want, and these days the most common scenarios involve data theft and ransomware. Now it's up to you (and the whole team) to identify what's happening, handle the incident, contain the attack and review the processes.

And I'll tell you with absolute certainty: relying on improvisation at times like these is the same as doing nothing. These are moments of high stress, chaos, and where every second counts.

Shall we go for an analogy? Imagine a doctor who knows all the theory, but has never performed a surgery, never watched one up close, and has no real practice. Would you let that doctor operate on your heart?

I doubt it! I wouldn't! Or think about a police officer who has never taken part in training or tactical simulations and now has to arrest the leader of the biggest cartel in the country.

These examples serve to provoke one (or two) important reflections:

- GRC is fundamental. Practice makes perfect, and guardrails exist to keep us from falling off the cliff.
- Empty policies and processes are the same as nothing. They're nothing more than paper.

That second point is critical. These days, a lot of GRC assessments reveal that most processes exist only to "tick the box" for the audit.

And don't get me wrong, it may be that this is all you're looking for right now.

But I guarantee you: being compliant with the audit is a long way from representing a good security posture. Paper doesn't stop attacks.

But the good news is: we're going to learn how to use that paper to the fullest, for management and for your team.

## What is this, anyway?

Cybersecurity is such a broad and extensive subject that I can comfortably state: there's a role close to cybersecurity for practically any skill.

GRC is one of those big chapters. But unlike the technical side we normally associate with security, GRC is much more oriented toward the administrative side.

Of all the subjects within cybersecurity, this is probably the most hated by many, and with good reason!

The way we create and apply these documents today is tedious, uninteresting, easy to ignore, and often feels like a never-ending, purposeless task.

But what few people realize is that all of this hides an extremely relevant and necessary universe.

(I know… it doesn't look like it!)

The concept of GRC goes far beyond cybersecurity. Although it's widely used in that context these days, GRC is also essential for areas like finance, business continuity, internal controls and audit, and all the others.

All of those sectors know the process well, even though the focus shifts a bit between them.

In this series, we're going to approach GRC with a focus on cybersecurity, even if some concepts overlap with other areas.

The term GRC officially emerged with the work of the Open Compliance and Ethics Group (OCEG), back in 2002.

They saw the need to integrate the three great pillars of modern management: Governance, Risk and Compliance.

Even though the term already existed informally, the first official publication on GRC only came in 2007.

But at the end of the day… what is GRC in practice? GRC is an essential process for the management and administration of any area.

The integration between Governance (decision-making), Risk (consequence) and Compliance (standards and obligations) allows for a strategic, comprehensive view of any operation. Let's use a simple example to understand the concept:

What keeps you from going around stealing everything you see in front of you?

Or what makes you go to the gym a few times a week?

You're making conscious decisions based on values and goals (governance), assessing the risks of those decisions (a heart attack at 50, for example), and respecting the rules of the environment you're in (like not punching someone off the leg press, or at least I hope not!).

This example may seem silly, but it illustrates what I mean:

GRC is already part of your life, whether you like it or not.

Entire philosophies have already discussed these same concepts applied to everyday life. After all, what's the difference between a child and an adult, if not the ability to weigh decisions, consequences, impacts… and, of course, pay the bills? (That part counts too.)

Now that the concept is clearer, it's time to understand how this process works in practice.

## How does this work in practice?

> "A journey of a thousand miles begins with a single step."
>
> Lao Tzu

If you're reading this post because you want to start, understand, build or study the GRC process, know one thing: every step counts, don't try to take on everything at once. GRC is a huge area, full of technical terms, corporate phrases (if you don't know yet what they sound like, rest assured you'll find out, nobody uses the phrase "in a timely manner" outside corporate contexts), and documents… hundreds of them.

But don't be scared. In this series, we're going to cover everything I think is important, so that in the end you'll be able to work in this area with more class or, at the very least, build that documentation the audit keeps demanding.

The first step is to understand your role. But what does that mean? GRC changes drastically depending on the context. You can't apply the same documents a software company uses to a bank. Sounds obvious, right? But the number of processes I've seen applied based on ready-made, generic templates is enormous. And here's my tip: don't do that! You only have two possible outcomes in that scenario:

- You apply documentation that's too restrictive or too complex and end up shooting yourself in the foot
- You create something far too generic, which is good for absolutely nothing

Use templates, but only as a direction. You need to create every term, policy and process with real meaning for your organization.

Back to your role, here are some questions that will help you:

- **What's your field of work?** Different areas require different documentation. E.g.: Development needs a secure development policy.
- **Which regulatory bodies oversee your field?** Who supervises what you do? Is there a specific entity? E.g.: the Central Bank for financial institutions.
- **Are there specific laws governing what you do?** Does any standard or legislation determine what can and can't be done? E.g.: LGPD.
- **What are the best practices for your most common processes?** What's widely recommended in your area? E.g.: Every new employee must sign a confidentiality agreement.
- **How big is the company?** This point is fundamental and frequently ignored (or used as an excuse). The size of your company determines when a process might be "too much" for the current structure. But careful: "this doesn't apply to me" can be a trap.

All of these questions are fundamental to creating a good GRC process. And of course, many other variables can come into play. As I said before: the process is complex, but the concepts are simple and you'll learn them easily. First, we isolate the points. Then we put everything together.

With the answers in hand and a clear objective, whether that's audit, compliance with standards or, in the best case, intelligent management of your resources, it's time to start the process itself.

We're going to look at each type of documentation over the course of this series, but to give you an idea of the path, here's a summary: The first step will probably be to create an Information Security Policy and a Cyber Security Policy. Those two are the most important, since they serve as a guide for all the others and you're unlikely to escape them, especially in today's world. With those two in hand, it's already possible to understand what the missing pieces are.

But so far we're only talking about policies. There are also the processes, and here are some questions to start thinking about:

- Which processes are critical?
- What makes a process critical to the operation?
- Does it depend on third parties or external suppliers?
- Does it depend on any system or software? What about hardware?
- Use this mindset: "which action or process, if interrupted, is going to cause a headache?"
- Which risks are associated with this process (briefly)?

Notice that the reasoning is the same one we used before: Before starting any documentation, understand the scenario in detail. That's fundamental to building something well designed.

With those answers, start mapping the processes and define:

- Which points are fundamental
- Which ones are optional
- Which steps can be improved

Example:

Process for hiring a new employee. What can't be missing from that process? Do they need to sign a confidentiality agreement? Go through training? Sign an employment contract? Receive their cards? Get their badge made?

This kind of mapping also brings an optimization perspective, and that's one of the great benefits of GRC: by documenting processes well, you see flaws and improvement opportunities that used to go unnoticed.

Now that we understand the basics, it's time to start a process from scratch.

In the next post, we're going to see how to kick off the GRC process in detail and, even better, how to integrate it into your day to day. The advantage? You'll be able to follow each post and build your process along with me.

I bet that if you put every step into practice, you'll come out with a super well-structured process. And if you're a student in the field, even better: you only learn by doing. Want to understand how an IT Asset Inventory Policy works? Then write one from scratch.
